Renders the frozen documents in docs/ (written by the build before the simplification) through the real engine in Chromium: `npm run harness -- --out run.json`.
Hashes of text-bearing renders depend on the installed fonts and the browser's rasteriser, so compare a commit against its parent on ONE machine (`git worktree add`, then `node scripts/harness/compare.mjs parent.json commit.json`); only text-free hashes are committed (text-free.sha256.json). Poster is the app's bundled face: the page loads it from the app's own stylesheet (src/styles/fonts.css) and waits for it as main.tsx does before it says it is ready, and run.mjs refuses a page where it did not load (`H.faces`).
From the commit that adds src/state/normalize.ts, each document is loaded through it exactly as an old save would be.
Besides the frozen documents, `default`, `backing-photo`, `small-2` and `small-4` are built live from the build under test (the default print, over the test photo, and at 20% / 40% type), and four that reach the corners of the press: `grain-photo` (grain over the photo, both plates with a second ink), `backing-second-ink` (a backing beside a second ink at its own switch point, turned), `press-max` (grain at screen 36, misregistration 30, edge 1, wear 0.8, type at 0.16 of the height) and `native-photo` (a 2400x3000 photo on 4:5: at height 3000 the export is the photo's own pixels). And four text-free frame documents (docs/frame-*.json, written for the frame, each with its photo's palette): `frame-original` (Original at the first photo's width, the photo's own colour), `frame-fit` (Fit on 4:5, the matte, round corners, the veil off), `frame-corners` (an Original portrait, round, on bare Paper) `frame-crop-zoomed` (1:1 at 200% and panned, the widest frame, half-round, Deep) and `frame-frosted` (Original, a wide frame, half-round, Frosted at Blur 120, the veil on; its field made, as the page makes it, from the test photo generated at the frame source's size rather than decoded, so its hash does not depend on the browser's decoder); their hashes are committed with text-free-photo's. Every document is one tile of the band engine's glyph grid at every height recorded (300, 1000, 1800): the 4:5 ones are 1440 px wide at 1800, the frame documents at most 2291x1800 (4.12 MP); a document of another shape needs its own check of that.
`--time doc@h` renders through the export's entry point (`renderPrintAsync`, no cache), so a timing is of what Save runs.
`--scale doc@h,...` renders each document at h and 2h and prints corr(box2(render(2h)), render(h)): 1 is the same print at both sizes.
`fidelity.html` / `fidelity.ts` is the page scripts/measure-fidelity.mjs drives: the real import (into this page's IndexedDB), the real decode for Save and the real `savePrint`, on generated zone plates, an EXIF-rotated JPEG and a PNG with a transparent hole, each saved file compared with the original drawn at its own pixels. The gates live in the script.
`--bands 1,7,97,full --heights 600,1000` renders every document through the export entry pressed in bands of each height (`renderPrintAsync`'s `bandRows`; `full` is one band) and exits 1 if any band height prints other bytes than the sheet pressed whole (bands of 1 and 7 only at heights up to 600). `H.render` refuses `bandRows` without `async`: the preview always presses its sheet whole.
The page also holds the save encoders' checks that scripts/check-encode.mjs drives (`H.encodeCheck`, `H.pngCheck`): the JPEG and PNG encoders on a gradient, a saturated halftone and a real print, decoded by the browser.
`--bands ... --tile 512,65536` does the same on a grid of forced 512x128 tiles (`H.render`'s `tile: [edge, pixels]`, the export's `tileBudget`), so a 1000 px sheet is 16 tiles and every band reads across tile rows. `H.render` refuses `tile` without `async` too.
`--seam doc@h,...` (P-4, tiles against one canvas): the document through the export entry on the tile grid (the real one, or `--tile`'s) and on a single tile as large as the sheet, compared pixel by pixel (`H.seam`). Over a photo at its own pixels, or none, that is the type's share; over a resampled photo the type's share is measured with the photo drawn once as one canvas draws it (`glyphsOnly`), and the run with the photo on the grid too is printed beside it. Reported: the differing pixels against the inked ones (coverage over 0 in any plane of the single-tile render), those over 128 levels, the plates whose box crosses a seam, and the differing pixels outside the widened boxes of those plates. It exits 1 outside the bounds: at most 1% of the inked pixels, at most 0.05% over 128 levels, none outside the boxes; a print with no type: at most a level, and on the real grid at most 0.1% of the bytes.
`H.fontCap()` measures a 9,800 px 'O' directly and as the engine measures type over its font cap, in every face; scripts/check-bands.mjs (CI) runs it, a forced-tile `--bands` subset and one `--seam` row.
`--file-parity 1000` (P-5, the file is the canvas): every document saved through the real `savePrint` as a PNG no taller than 1000 px, decoded, against the export entry painting the same print into a canvas at that height (`H.fileParity`); exits 1 on any difference.
`--chain` (the whole chain, in Frosted, the one fill made from the photo's own pixels at Save): a photo imported for real into the page's IndexedDB, its palette made from it by the page's maker and stored with it (and through the normaliser), a document with a plate over it in Frosted; the preview's inputs made as the page's preview makes them (`previewFrameInputs`: the frame source decoded from the stored file, the settled field) and Save's as Save makes them (`ensureFrameInputs`), which must be the very field kept. The file is the one the app's own Save writes: `pressForSave` with the page's makers (it makes the inputs, decodes the original and presses), as a PNG, at the photo's own size (an 1800x1350 photo on Original, a 2250x1800 sheet). The preview's render, the export's and that file, decoded, must be the same bytes (`H.chain`); exits 1 if not. A plan below the photo's own size, or at another height, is refused: there Save presses a smaller copy decoded resized by the browser, which the preview's scaled photo matches only to a level, so a change to the planner cannot move the chain off Save's native path unseen.
`--retry` (P-6): the real `savePrint` with a `RangeError` injected once into the typed-array constructors, at set-up (the first array of a megabyte) and mid-press (the first of 256 KB once the second band has begun), against the same save with none: the same file, pressed again in more, shorter bands (`H.retry`); exits 1 otherwise.
`--frame-cost` (the frame's cost in the preview): `native-photo` with its own photo and a 12 MP photo (4032x3024 on 4:5) from the working copy the preview decodes of it, framed in Crop and in Fit (the first photo's width, round corners, the photo's own colour) and in Frosted at Blur 60 (Crop, its field made once and kept, as the page keeps it), each at the settled and the draft size, rendered as the preview renders a frame (its canvases and cache kept, the photo drawn and the frame composed every time), against the same document unframed (what a person waits for) and against the same framed render with its compose skipped (`H.frameCost`'s `bare`, the preview's test seam `skipFrameCompose`: the photo drawn into the same rectangle, so the ratio is the frame's own work; in Fit the photo is drawn smaller than unframed, which would hide it). A sample is one render. The four documents and sizes are interleaved round by round, so each one's 15 rounds (`--runs n`) spread over the whole measurement; a draft round is 10 samples of each (a draft render is ~8 ms). Within a round the ten renders run in a cycle, started one further on each round and reversed every other round, and the first render of each round's turn is not timed (the first render after another document or size ran 2 to 6% slower). A render's time is the mean of its fastest samples, a fifth of one measurement's (3 settled, 30 draft): interference only adds time, and on a shared machine it comes in spells that can outlast a document's rounds taken back to back. A document and size with a row (Crop, Fit, Frosted, Frosted made again, or Frosted made for a new shape) over its bound is measured again, up to twice, its new samples added to its old, so a spell in one measurement is outweighed by the next, whichever render it slowed; every measurement is printed and kept in the JSON (`frameCost[...].measurements`: the verdict on all the samples so far, and that measurement's own samples in `runs`; the final verdict in `.ok`). And Frosted with its field made again inside the render (`H.frameCost`'s `remake: true`: a blur one off the last, the field made in the timed render from the kept cover through the page's `frostFields`, as each frame of a Blur drag makes it), against the same document unframed. And Frosted on Original (`frosted-original`, the document's sheet set to Original) with its field made for a new sheet shape inside the render (`remake: 'reshape'`: the next of six widths a few hundred-thousandths apart, each its own sheet shape on the same canvas, so the render cache stays and the cover and the field are both made, as each frame of a Width drag there makes them; the rig's `frostFields` counts what it makes, and a step that makes no cover or no field, or a Blur step no field, throws, so a page that keeps more covers or fields than the drag steps through fails the run instead of timing kept renders), against the same render with its field kept (`frosted-original`). Exits 1 if a framed render still takes more than 1.10x either settled, 1.25x draft, the remade Frosted more than 1.58x settled, 1.60x draft, or the reshaped one more than 1.82x settled, 4.03x draft its field kept (each what it measured x 1.25), after the third measurement.
`H.parityBorders` is the page scripts/parity-borders.mjs drives: one of Border Studio's frozen frames (scripts/parity-borders/), its block photo and its keyed hex, rendered by the export's entry with the veil off and no type, and the reference PNG decoded, both returned as RGBA; the gates live in the script.
